body:
`paper.labs` is also configured as the inverse side (`Lab.papers` owns the `@JoinTable()`), so setting `paper.labs = [...]` here likely won’t write the Paper↔Lab join table when saving the Paper. To ensure the mapping is persisted, update the owning side (`mappedLab.papers`) or use a relation query builder to add the relation.
```suggestion
    let mappedLab: Lab | null = null;

    // We assume the creator is a Coordinator (enforced by frontend/role checks)
    const coordinator = await coordinatorRepo.findOne({ where: { id: creator.id } });
    if (coordinator) {
        paper.coordinators = [coordinator];
        // Automatically map this paper to the Coordinator's Lab
        mappedLab = await labRepo.findOne({ where: { coordinator: { id: coordinator.id } } });
        paper.labs = mappedLab ? [mappedLab] : [];
    } else {
        paper.coordinators = [];
        paper.labs = [];
    }

    // Save the paper first, then persist the Lab relation from the owning side.
    const savedPaper = await paperRepo.save(paper);

    if (mappedLab) {
      await AppDataSource
        .createQueryBuilder()
        .relation(Lab, 'papers')
        .of(mappedLab)
        .add(savedPaper);

      savedPaper.labs = [mappedLab];
    }

    return savedPaper;
```
